course: dictionary
lesson: 12025
title: Two meanings of choirboy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A choirboy is a boy who sings in a church or cathedral choir, usually wearing robes. You can often spot the church meaning from the words around it. «The choirboys sang at the Christmas service in the cathedral» tells you the setting is a church.
explain: Sometimes the word is used in a joking way. «Ben is no choirboy: he is always up to mischief» does not mean Ben sings in a choir. It means Ben is not innocent, even though some people might expect him to be.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Does «He looks like a choirboy» still make sense if you think of innocent and well-behaved? Yes, so the joking meaning fits. Always check which meaning suits the words around it.
fact: A choirboy is a boy who sings in a church or cathedral choir.
fact: Choirboys often wear robes and sing at church services, especially at Christmas.
fact: Choirboys often sing high parts called treble.
fact: Informally, «he looks like a choirboy» means he looks innocent and well-behaved, though he may not be.
fact: «No choirboy» means someone who is not innocent, often said jokingly.
exercise: «The choirboys sang at the Christmas service in the cathedral.» What is a choirboy? | *A boy who sings in a church or cathedral choir | A boy who sells sweets outside the church | A boy who builds cathedrals | A boy who rings the church bells
exercise: «Ben is no choirboy: he is always up to mischief.» What does «no choirboy» mean? | *Not innocent, often up to mischief | A very quiet boy who never speaks | A boy who lives in a cathedral | A boy who sings beautifully
exercise: In a story, «he looks like a choirboy» suggests what? | Someone who is very tall | Someone who is always cold | *Someone who looks sweet and innocent, though he may not be | Someone who loves sport and running
test: «The choirboys sang the high treble parts at St Paul's.» Which voice part did they sing? | Tenor | Alto | Bass | *Treble, the high parts
test: «The little choirboy looked like an angel, but he had just stolen the biscuits.» What does the sentence suggest? | The boy was a real angel from heaven | The boy was singing in a choir | The boy was very tall | *The boy seemed innocent but was not
exam: Where do choirboys often sing at Christmas? | In a bakery | In a petrol station | *In a cathedral | In a sports hall
